insert into questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('n-q-300','netplus',3,'3.1','mcq',1,'A technician joining the team needs to learn which VLANs exist, which IP subnet each one uses, and how traffic is routed between them. Which piece of documentation should they be given?','["The logical network diagram", "The physical network diagram", "The rack elevation diagram", "The cable map for the main distribution frame"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A logical diagram depicts the network as addressing and forwarding see it: VLAN IDs, IP subnets, routing boundaries, firewall zones and traffic flow, independent of where the hardware physically sits. The physical diagram is the tempting choice because it also shows devices, but it documents cable runs, port assignments and equipment locations - it will tell you which closet a switch lives in, not which subnet rides on it.'),
('n-q-301','netplus',3,'3.1','mcq',2,'A data centre team must decide whether a new 2U firewall will fit in an existing cabinet, and whether that cabinet has power and cooling headroom for it. Which document answers this most directly?','["The rack diagram (rack elevation)", "The logical network diagram", "The IP address management database", "The service-level agreement with the colocation provider"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A rack diagram is a front and rear elevation showing which rack units are occupied by which device, plus power feeds, weight and cooling notes - exactly the free-space and capacity question being asked. The SLA is the tempting distractor because a colocation contract does cap power draw per cabinet, but it states the contractual limit, not which rack units are currently free.'),
('n-q-302','netplus',3,'3.1','mcq',2,'Two projects independently allocated 10.20.30.0/24 to different sites, and several servers were later found sharing addresses with statically configured printers. Which practice would have prevented this?','["Maintaining an IP address management (IPAM) system of record", "Increasing the DHCP lease time at both sites", "Enabling DHCP snooping on the access switches", "Adding a second DHCP scope at each site"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'IPAM is the authoritative record of which subnets are allocated to which sites and which individual addresses are assigned, reserved or free - it is the control that stops two teams claiming the same range and stops a static host colliding with a dynamic one. DHCP snooping is the tempting distractor, but it only blocks unauthorised DHCP servers; it has no visibility of statically assigned addresses or of subnet allocation across sites.'),
('n-q-303','netplus',3,'3.1','mcq',1,'A carrier contract commits to 99.9 percent circuit availability and a four-hour response to a reported outage, with billing credits if either is missed. What is this document called?','["A service-level agreement (SLA)", "A memorandum of understanding (MOU)", "An acceptable use policy (AUP)", "A non-disclosure agreement (NDA)"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'An SLA is the contractual statement of the service levels a provider commits to - availability percentage, response and repair targets - together with the remedy when they are missed. An MOU is the tempting choice because it is also an agreement between parties, but it records intent and mutual understanding and is typically non-binding with no measurable service targets or penalties.'),
('n-q-304','netplus',3,'3.1','mcq',2,'Warehouse staff report dead zones near the loading bays. Before adding access points, the team wants a floor-plan overlay showing measured signal strength throughout the building. What should they perform?','["An on-site wireless survey producing a heat map", "A spectrum analysis to identify non-802.11 interference", "A packet capture at the wireless controller", "A rack audit of the wiring closets"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A wireless site survey walks the floor collecting measured signal readings and renders them as a heat map over the floor plan, which is precisely how coverage gaps and AP placement are determined. Spectrum analysis is the tempting distractor and is a valid wireless tool, but it shows RF energy and interference sources across the band - it will not tell you where your own SSID coverage falls off.'),
('n-q-305','netplus',3,'3.1','mcq',2,'A vendor announced that a switch model reached end-of-life two years ago and will reach end-of-support in six months. What is the significance of the end-of-support date?','["The vendor will stop issuing security patches and firmware fixes, so newly discovered vulnerabilities will remain unremediated", "The switches will stop forwarding traffic and must be powered off on that date", "The vendor will stop selling the model to new customers", "Existing support contracts automatically convert to a next-business-day hardware warranty"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'End-of-support is the date after which the vendor issues no further patches, firmware or technical assistance - the device keeps working, but every vulnerability found from then on is permanent, which is what forces a refresh or compensating controls. Stopping sales to new customers is the tempting confusion: that is end-of-life, and in this scenario it already happened two years ago.'),
('n-q-306','netplus',3,'3.1','mcq',3,'A retired branch router is being removed from service and sent to an asset disposal vendor. Which action most directly addresses the confidentiality risk created by the disposal?','["Erase the stored configuration, keys and credentials from the device before it leaves the building", "Remove the device from the rack diagram and cable map", "Cancel the maintenance contract for the device", "Release the device management address back into the DHCP scope"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A retired router still holds its running and startup configurations: VPN pre-shared keys, SNMP community strings, RADIUS secrets and interface addressing that map the internal network. Sanitising that before the asset leaves custody is the confidentiality control. Updating documentation is a genuine decommissioning step and is the tempting answer, but stale diagrams cause operational confusion, not data disclosure.'),
('n-q-307','netplus',3,'3.1','mcq',1,'Which element of a change request describes the predefined steps for returning the system to its last known good state if the change does not work as expected?','["The rollback (backout) plan", "The maintenance window", "The impact assessment", "The change advisory board approval"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'The rollback plan is the documented, tested path back to the prior working state, and it is what makes it safe to attempt a change inside a limited window. The maintenance window is the tempting answer because it is when you would execute a rollback, but it is only the agreed period of time - it says nothing about how to undo the change.'),
('n-q-308','netplus',3,'3.1','mcq',3,'Twenty access switches that should be identical have accumulated small differences in VLAN, logging and hardening settings. The team wants one approved reference to compare every switch against. What should they establish?','["A baseline (golden) configuration for the switch role", "A nightly backup of each switch configuration", "A longer DHCP lease time on the management VLAN", "A separate change request for each switch"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A baseline or golden configuration is the approved standard for a device role; comparing production configurations against it is what detects and quantifies configuration drift. Nightly configuration backups are the tempting distractor and are good practice, but a backup faithfully preserves whatever was on the device - including the drift - so it gives you a restore point, not a standard of correctness.'),
('n-q-309','netplus',3,'3.1','mcq',3,'A technician adds an access control list to a switch and confirms it works. After a power outage that night, the ACL is gone. What most likely happened?','["The change was applied to the running configuration but never saved to the startup configuration", "The change advisory board revoked the change automatically", "The switch reached its end-of-support date and reverted to factory defaults", "The baseline configuration was pushed to the switch by the DHCP server"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'The active configuration lives in volatile memory and is lost at power-off; it survives a reboot only if it is written to the startup configuration in non-volatile storage. Restoration from a stored baseline is the tempting distractor, but that is a deliberate action a person or an automation tool performs - it does not happen spontaneously during a power outage, and DHCP servers do not push device configurations.'),
('n-q-310','netplus',3,'3.1','multi',2,'Which THREE items should a network change request contain before it is submitted for approval? (Choose three.)','["An assessment of the impact and risk to affected users and services", "A rollback plan describing how to reverse the change", "A proposed implementation window and expected duration", "The administrative credentials for every affected device", "The original purchase price of the affected hardware", "A copy of the vendor end-of-life notice"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1, 2]'::jsonb,'Approvers need to know what the change will affect and how badly it could go wrong, how it will be undone, and when it will happen - impact and risk, rollback plan and proposed window are the core of a change record. Credentials are the dangerous inclusion: they belong in a privileged credential store, and circulating them through a ticketing system is itself a security failure.'),
('n-q-311','netplus',3,'3.1','multi',3,'Which TWO pieces of information appear on a logical network diagram but not on a physical one? (Choose two.)','["VLAN identifiers and the IP subnet assigned to each", "The routing relationships and traffic flow between segments", "The rack unit position of each device", "The patch panel port that serves each wall jack", "The measured length of each fibre run"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1]'::jsonb,'Logical diagrams describe addressing and forwarding - VLANs, subnets, routing adjacencies, firewall zones and how traffic moves between them. Rack positions, patch panel mappings and cable lengths are all physical facts belonging on rack diagrams and cable maps; the frequent mistake is expecting one drawing to serve both audiences, which is exactly why the objective treats them as two separate artefacts.'),
('n-q-312','netplus',3,'3.1','multi',2,'Which THREE actions belong in a proper decommissioning procedure for a network device? (Choose three.)','["Sanitise the stored configuration and any cached credentials or keys", "Remove its records from IPAM, DNS and the monitoring system", "Update the rack diagram, cable map and asset inventory", "Leave its uplink patched in case the device is reinstalled later", "Ask the vendor to extend the end-of-support date", "Raise its SNMP polling frequency to capture final statistics"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1, 2]'::jsonb,'Decommissioning has three strands: destroy the data the device holds, remove the entries that would otherwise generate false alerts and stale name resolution, and correct the documentation so the next technician is not chasing a ghost. Leaving the uplink patched is the tempting shortcut, but a live, unmonitored port on a decommissioned run is exactly the kind of forgotten entry point that surfaces in a later audit.'),
('n-q-313','netplus',3,'3.1','ordering',2,'Place the stages of a standard change management process in the correct order.','["Submit a change request documenting the scope, purpose and impact", "Obtain approval from the change advisory board or designated approver", "Schedule the work inside an approved maintenance window", "Implement the change with the rollback plan ready", "Validate and test that the change achieved its stated purpose", "Update the affected documentation and close the change record"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]'::jsonb,'A change is requested and assessed, then approved, then scheduled for a low-impact window, then implemented with a rollback path available, then validated, and only then documented and closed. The common exam trap is implementing before approval, or closing the record before validation - an unvalidated change marked complete is how silent outages reach the next business day.'),
('n-q-314','netplus',3,'3.1','matching',2,'Match each documentation artefact to the question it is designed to answer.','{"left": ["Rack diagram", "Cable map", "Logical network diagram", "Wireless heat map", "IPAM database"], "right": ["Which patch panel port feeds a given wall jack or switch port", "How strong is the wireless signal in each area of the floor plan", "Which VLANs and subnets exist and how traffic routes between them", "Which rack unit does a device occupy and what is its power draw", "Which addresses in a subnet are already allocated, and to what"]}'::jsonb,'[3, 0, 2, 1, 4]'::jsonb,'Each artefact answers a different question: rack diagrams cover physical placement and capacity, cable maps the copper or fibre path between endpoints, logical diagrams addressing and forwarding, heat maps measured RF coverage, and IPAM address allocation. The usual error is conflating the rack diagram with the cable map - one records what occupies each rack unit, the other records what each port connects to.'),
('n-q-315','netplus',3,'3.2','mcq',3,'A monitoring team discovers that alerts about interface flaps sometimes never reach the manager, and there is no way to tell whether one was lost in transit. Which SNMP mechanism addresses this?','["Use SNMP informs, which the manager acknowledges and the agent retransmits if unacknowledged", "Use SNMPv3 encryption so the alert cannot be dropped by intermediate devices", "Increase the polling frequency so more Get requests are issued", "Move the trap receiver from UDP 162 to UDP 161"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A trap is a fire-and-forget UDP notification: if it is dropped, neither side knows. An inform is the acknowledged variant - the manager confirms receipt and the agent retransmits until it does, which is exactly the reliability gap described. Encryption is the tempting distractor because SNMPv3 is the right answer to so many SNMP questions, but privacy and authentication protect confidentiality and integrity, not delivery.'),
('n-q-316','netplus',3,'3.2','mcq',2,'A router rebooted twice overnight, but the monitoring system that polls it every five minutes shows continuous availability. What is the best way to capture events like this in future?','["Configure the device to send SNMP traps so the agent reports the event the moment it occurs", "Reduce the polling interval to sixty seconds and accept the extra load", "Enable NetFlow export on the router uplinks", "Add the router to a scheduled network discovery job"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Polling is sampling: anything that begins and ends between two samples is invisible, no matter how the interval is tuned. Traps are agent-initiated and fire at the moment of the event, so a reboot or interface transition is reported rather than inferred. Shortening the interval is the tempting fix, but it only narrows the blind spot while multiplying query load - it never eliminates it.'),
('n-q-317','netplus',3,'3.2','mcq',3,'An engineer needs traffic visibility across a multi-vendor 40 Gbps core. Exact byte counts for every conversation are not required, and forwarding performance must not be affected. Which approach fits best?','["sFlow, which exports a statistical 1-in-N packet sample from the switching hardware", "Full packet capture on each core uplink, retained for thirty days", "A SPAN session mirroring every core port to a single analyser port", "Polling SNMP interface counters once per second on every core port"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'sFlow samples packets at a fixed rate in hardware and is vendor-neutral, giving representative traffic composition at very high link speeds with negligible overhead - the right trade when trends matter more than exact totals. Mirroring every core port is the tempting alternative, but the aggregate of several 40 Gbps ports vastly oversubscribes a single mirror destination, so the analyser silently drops traffic and the data becomes untrustworthy.'),
('n-q-318','netplus',3,'3.2','mcq',2,'Firewall, directory and IDS logs are all forwarded to a central syslog collector, but nobody notices when a failed VPN login is followed minutes later by a privilege change on a server. What capability is missing?','["A SIEM that correlates events across sources and alerts on the combined pattern", "A larger disk on the syslog collector so older logs are retained", "Syslog forwarding over TCP instead of UDP", "A lower syslog severity threshold so debug messages are captured"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A syslog collector centralises and stores messages; a SIEM adds normalisation, cross-source correlation, alerting and search, which is what turns two unremarkable events on two systems into one actionable detection. Reliable TCP transport is the tempting improvement and does reduce message loss, but delivering the same uncorrelated messages more reliably still leaves nobody joining them together.'),
('n-q-319','netplus',3,'3.2','mcq',3,'A static alert threshold of 70 percent WAN utilisation fires every night during backups and every fortnight during payroll processing, and the team has begun ignoring it. What is the best remedy?','["Baseline the link over a period covering the full business cycle and alert on deviation from the expected pattern", "Raise the static threshold to 95 percent so routine peaks stop alerting", "Disable utilisation alerting and rely on user reports", "Shorten the polling interval so the peaks are sampled more precisely"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Alert fatigue here is caused by comparing one fixed number against traffic that is legitimately cyclical. Baselining across a representative period - including the nightly and monthly peaks - lets the system flag genuine anomalies rather than expected behaviour. Raising the threshold to 95 percent is the tempting quick fix, but silencing the nightly peak also blinds you to a real 90 percent saturation event.'),
('n-q-320','netplus',3,'3.2','multi',3,'Which THREE capabilities does SNMPv3 add that SNMPv2c does not provide? (Choose three.)','["Authentication of the message source rather than a shared cleartext community string", "Encryption (privacy) of the message payload", "Message integrity checking to detect modification in transit", "The GetBulk operation for efficient retrieval of large tables", "Automatic discovery of every device on the subnet", "Delivery of notifications over TCP instead of UDP"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1, 2]'::jsonb,'SNMPv3 introduces the user-based security model, giving the three security services v2c lacks: authentication, integrity and optional privacy, selectable as noAuthNoPriv, authNoPriv or authPriv. GetBulk is the tempting inclusion because it is a genuine improvement over v1, but it arrived with v2c; v3 is a security revision, not a performance one, and it does not change the UDP transport.'),
('n-q-321','netplus',3,'3.2','matching',2,'Match each monitoring capability to what it provides.','{"left": ["Availability monitoring", "Performance monitoring", "Configuration monitoring", "Network discovery", "Port mirroring (SPAN)"], "right": ["Detects when a running configuration has drifted from the approved baseline", "Reports whether a device or service is reachable and responding right now", "Builds and maintains an inventory of devices and how they interconnect", "Delivers a copy of selected switch traffic to an analyser or IDS sensor", "Tracks utilisation, latency, errors and other quality metrics over time"]}'::jsonb,'[1, 4, 0, 2, 3]'::jsonb,'Availability answers up or down, performance answers how well, configuration monitoring answers whether the device still matches its approved standard, discovery answers what exists, and port mirroring is the plumbing that feeds packet-level tools. Availability and performance monitoring are the pair most often conflated: a device can answer every poll while the link it serves is saturated and unusable.'),
('n-q-322','netplus',3,'3.3','mcq',2,'A business states that in a disaster it can tolerate losing at most fifteen minutes of transaction data. Which disaster recovery metric has been defined?','["Recovery point objective (RPO)", "Recovery time objective (RTO)", "Mean time to repair (MTTR)", "Mean time between failures (MTBF)"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'RPO measures backwards from the moment of the incident: it is the maximum age of the data you are willing to recover to, and therefore the maximum acceptable data loss. RTO is the classic confusion, but it measures forwards from the incident - how long the service may stay down - and says nothing about how much data is lost.'),
('n-q-323','netplus',3,'3.3','mcq',2,'After an outage, management requires the order-entry system to be serving customers again within two hours. Which metric does this two-hour figure express?','["Recovery time objective (RTO)", "Recovery point objective (RPO)", "Mean time to repair (MTTR)", "Mean time between failures (MTBF)"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'RTO is the maximum tolerable duration of the outage itself - the deadline for having service restored - and it drives the recovery architecture you must buy. MTTR is the tempting answer because it is also expressed as time to restore, but MTTR is a historical average of how long repairs actually take, not a target the business negotiates.'),
('n-q-324','netplus',3,'3.3','mcq',3,'A network manager is comparing two firewall models and wants evidence about how often each is likely to fail, rather than how quickly it can be fixed. Which figure should be compared?','["Mean time between failures (MTBF)", "Mean time to repair (MTTR)", "Recovery time objective (RTO)", "Recovery point objective (RPO)"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'MTBF is the average operating time between failures of a repairable system and is the reliability figure - a higher MTBF means failures are rarer. MTTR is the tempting choice because both are mean-time metrics, but MTTR describes maintainability: how long it takes to get a failed unit back into service once it has already failed.'),
('n-q-325','netplus',3,'3.3','mcq',3,'A company tightens the RPO for a database from twenty-four hours to fifteen minutes. Which change most directly satisfies the new requirement?','["Replicate or snapshot the data far more frequently, at least every fifteen minutes", "Purchase faster restore hardware so a full restore completes more quickly", "Add a second administrator to the on-call rotation", "Select a storage array with a higher MTBF rating"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'RPO is set by how recent your last good copy is, so it is satisfied by backup or replication frequency - nothing else moves it. Faster restore hardware is the tempting answer because it clearly improves recovery, but it shortens how long recovery takes, which is the RTO; restoring a twenty-four-hour-old backup very quickly still loses twenty-four hours of data.'),
('n-q-326','netplus',3,'3.3','mcq',3,'An organisation needs an alternate site that can take over within roughly a day, and cannot justify the cost of continuously replicated infrastructure. Which site type fits?','["A warm site with hardware and connectivity in place, requiring data restoration and final cutover", "A hot site with fully configured systems and near-real-time replicated data", "A cold site consisting of conditioned space, power and network drops only", "No alternate site, relying on off-site backup media alone"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A warm site already has the equipment, circuits and a baseline configuration, so recovery is a matter of restoring current data and completing cutover - typically hours up to about a day, at a fraction of hot-site cost. A hot site would meet the deadline comfortably but is exactly the continuously replicated expense being ruled out, and a cold site takes far longer than a day because equipment must still be procured, installed and configured.'),
('n-q-327','netplus',3,'3.3','mcq',2,'A trading platform must resume operation within minutes of losing its primary data centre, with essentially no data loss. Which recovery site is required?','["A hot site", "A warm site", "A cold site", "An off-site backup vault"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A hot site is fully provisioned, configured and kept current through continuous or near-continuous replication, so it can assume production load in minutes - the only option that satisfies both a minutes-long RTO and a near-zero RPO. A warm site is the tempting compromise, but the pause to restore data and finish cutover puts recovery in the hours range.'),
('n-q-328','netplus',3,'3.3','mcq',3,'Two firewalls run active-active, each normally carrying about 60 percent of its rated capacity. What should the design team be most concerned about?','["If one unit fails, the survivor must carry the entire load and will be oversubscribed", "Active-active pairs cannot share session state, so all connections drop on failover", "The passive unit will consume licences it never uses", "Active-active requires the two units to sit in different data centres"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Active-active shares load across both nodes, so the sizing rule is that each node must be able to absorb the total load alone - at 60 percent each, the survivor faces 120 percent and will drop traffic. The claim that active-active pairs cannot share session state is the plausible-sounding trap: state synchronisation is a normal feature of both active-active and active-passive clusters.'),
('n-q-329','netplus',3,'3.3','mcq',2,'A legacy application writes to a local database that cannot tolerate two instances writing at the same time, but the business still needs rapid recovery from a node failure. Which high-availability design suits it?','["Active-passive, because only one node processes work while the standby stays synchronised and ready", "Active-active, because sharing the load across both nodes reduces the chance of failure", "Active-active with a load balancer using round-robin distribution", "No clustering at all, relying on restoring the nightly backup"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Active-passive fits any workload that cannot safely run in two places at once: one node owns the work, the standby is kept current and takes over on failure, so recovery is fast without ever having two writers. Active-active is the tempting answer because it uses the idle capacity, but distributing writes across both nodes is precisely what this application cannot survive.'),
('n-q-330','netplus',3,'3.3','mcq',1,'The incident response and network teams sit in a conference room and talk through their roles and decisions for a simulated ransomware outage, without touching any production or recovery system. What type of exercise is this?','["A tabletop exercise", "A validation test", "A live failover test", "A vulnerability assessment"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A tabletop exercise is discussion-based: participants walk through the plan to surface gaps, unclear ownership and bad assumptions, with no systems involved and therefore no operational risk. A validation test is the tempting alternative, but by definition it exercises the actual recovery mechanisms rather than talking about them.'),
('n-q-331','netplus',3,'3.3','mcq',2,'A disaster recovery plan has passed three consecutive tabletop reviews. Management now wants proof that the recovery site can actually run the business. What should be scheduled?','["A validation test that performs the real failover and confirms services and data at the recovery site", "A further tabletop exercise with a wider group of participants", "A review of the SLA with the colocation provider", "An adjustment of the recovery point objective"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Only a validation test exercises the mechanisms themselves - failing workloads over, then verifying that applications, data currency and dependencies genuinely work at the recovery site. Another tabletop is the tempting low-risk option, but a plan can read perfectly and still fail on an expired certificate, a missing firewall rule or a dependency nobody documented.'),
('n-q-332','netplus',3,'3.3','multi',2,'Which TWO of the following are objectives negotiated with the business rather than measured historical averages? (Choose two.)','["Recovery point objective (RPO)", "Recovery time objective (RTO)", "Mean time to repair (MTTR)", "Mean time between failures (MTBF)", "Maximum transmission unit (MTU)"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1]'::jsonb,'RPO and RTO are targets: the business decides how much data loss and how much downtime it can absorb, and the recovery design is then built to meet them. MTTR and MTBF are the tempting inclusions because they are also DR metrics, but they are observed averages drawn from failure history or vendor testing - you influence them over time, you do not simply declare them.'),
('n-q-333','netplus',3,'3.3','multi',2,'Which THREE statements describe a cold site? (Choose three.)','["It provides conditioned space, power, cooling and network access but little or no installed equipment", "Equipment must be procured, installed and configured after the disaster is declared", "It has the longest recovery time of the three standard site types", "It holds near-real-time replicated copies of production data", "It is the most expensive of the three standard site types to maintain", "It can absorb production load within minutes of a declaration"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1, 2]'::jsonb,'A cold site is a shell: the facility exists, but the systems, data and configuration do not, so recovery means building the environment after the event - the cheapest option to hold and the slowest to use. Replicated data and a minutes-long cutover describe a hot site, which sits at the opposite end of the same cost-versus-speed trade-off.'),
('n-q-334','netplus',3,'3.3','ordering',2,'Place the phases of a disaster recovery response in the correct order.','["Declare the disaster according to the plan and notify the response team", "Fail production workloads over to the recovery site", "Verify that critical services and data are functioning correctly at the recovery site", "Communicate the restoration status to users and business stakeholders", "Fail back to the primary site once it has been repaired and validated", "Hold a post-incident review and update the disaster recovery plan"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]'::jsonb,'Formal declaration comes first because it authorises the team to act, then failover, then verification before any restoration claim is made, then stakeholder communication, then a controlled fail-back once the primary is proven healthy, and finally the lessons-learned update. Announcing restoration before verifying it is the classic sequencing error - it sends users back into a site that is not actually ready.'),
('n-q-335','netplus',3,'3.3','matching',3,'Match each disaster recovery metric to its definition.','{"left": ["RPO", "RTO", "MTTR", "MTBF"], "right": ["The average elapsed time to restore a failed component to service", "The maximum tolerable age of the data you recover to, and therefore the maximum acceptable data loss", "The average operating time between failures of a repairable system", "The maximum tolerable duration of the outage before service must be restored"]}'::jsonb,'[1, 3, 0, 2]'::jsonb,'RPO looks backwards from the incident at data currency, RTO looks forwards at downtime, MTTR is the measured repair time for a failed component, and MTBF is the measured reliability interval between failures. RTO and MTTR are the pair most often swapped: RTO is a deadline the business sets, while MTTR is what your repair history says you actually achieve.'),
('n-q-336','netplus',3,'3.4','mcq',2,'A subnet uses the scope 192.168.10.1 to 192.168.10.254, and the addresses .1 through .20 are manually configured on routers, printers and servers that do not use DHCP. What should be configured so the DHCP server never issues those addresses?','["An exclusion range covering 192.168.10.1 to 192.168.10.20", "A reservation for each of the twenty addresses", "A shorter lease time on the scope", "A second scope beginning at 192.168.10.21"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'An exclusion tells the server to skip a block of addresses inside the scope, which is exactly right for hosts that are statically configured and never send a DHCP request. Reservations are the tempting answer and would also stop the addresses being handed to others, but a reservation binds an address to a specific client identifier and is meaningful only for a client that actually uses DHCP - creating twenty of them for static hosts is fragile busywork.'),
('n-q-337','netplus',3,'3.4','mcq',3,'A monitoring appliance must always receive the same IP address, but the team wants its DNS and NTP server settings to update automatically when those change centrally. What is the best configuration?','["A DHCP reservation bound to the appliance MAC address", "A static IP address configured on the appliance itself", "An exclusion for the appliance address plus a static configuration", "A shorter lease time so the appliance renews more often"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A reservation gives the client a permanently consistent address while it continues to obtain everything else from DHCP, so changes to scope options such as DNS and NTP servers propagate at the next renewal. A static address on the host is the tempting equivalent, but it pins the option values on the device too, and every future DNS or NTP change becomes a manual visit.'),
('n-q-338','netplus',3,'3.4','mcq',2,'A conference venue guest network runs out of DHCP addresses by mid-afternoon, even though a scan shows only a fraction of the scope actively in use. What is the most appropriate fix?','["Shorten the lease time so addresses from departed devices return to the pool sooner", "Lengthen the lease time to reduce DHCP traffic", "Enable a DHCP relay on the guest VLAN", "Create reservations for the most frequent guests"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'In a high-turnover environment, long leases keep addresses bound to devices that left hours ago, so the pool depletes while most bindings are stale - shortening the lease returns them quickly. Lengthening the lease is the tempting instinct for reducing DHCP chatter, but it makes exactly this problem worse.'),
('n-q-339','netplus',3,'3.4','mcq',2,'Clients on a new VLAN receive valid addresses and masks from DHCP and can ping each other, but cannot reach any other subnet or resolve any name. What should be checked first?','["The scope options for the default gateway and DNS servers", "Whether the scope has been exhausted", "Whether the clients support SLAAC", "The lease duration configured on the scope"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Correct addressing on the local subnet with no off-subnet reachability and no name resolution is the signature of missing or wrong scope options - the router and DNS server options in particular. Scope exhaustion is the tempting distractor, but an exhausted scope produces clients with no lease at all, typically self-assigning an APIPA address, rather than clients holding a valid one.'),
('n-q-340','netplus',3,'3.4','mcq',2,'An IPv6 host on a network with no DHCPv6 server obtains a routable global address by combining the prefix advertised by the router with a locally generated interface identifier. What is this called?','["Stateless address autoconfiguration (SLAAC)", "Stateful DHCPv6 address assignment", "Automatic private IP addressing (APIPA)", "Link-local address generation"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'SLAAC is defined by exactly this behaviour: the host learns the prefix from a router advertisement and forms its own address, with duplicate address detection confirming uniqueness - no server holds any binding. Link-local generation is the tempting distractor because it is also automatic and serverless, but it always produces an fe80::/10 address that is not routable off the link.'),
('n-q-341','netplus',3,'3.4','mcq',3,'A network should let IPv6 hosts build their own addresses via SLAAC while still learning DNS server addresses from a central DHCPv6 server. How should the router advertisements be configured?','["Set the O (other configuration) flag so hosts use SLAAC for addressing and stateless DHCPv6 for other settings", "Set the M (managed) flag so hosts request their addresses from DHCPv6", "Clear both the M and O flags so hosts rely entirely on the router advertisement", "Disable router advertisements so hosts fall back to DHCPv6"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'With the prefix advertised for SLAAC and the O flag set, hosts self-assign an address but query DHCPv6 for other parameters such as DNS - the stateless DHCPv6 model the scenario describes. Setting the M flag is the tempting choice because it also involves DHCPv6, but it makes the server assign the addresses too, which is the stateful model the scenario deliberately avoids.'),
('n-q-342','netplus',3,'3.4','mcq',2,'To let receiving mail systems verify which servers may send mail for the company domain, an administrator must publish an SPF policy. Which DNS record type carries it?','["TXT", "MX", "PTR", "CNAME"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'SPF and DKIM policies and domain-ownership verification strings are all published as TXT records - free-form text a domain owner publishes for other systems to read. MX is the tempting choice because the topic is mail, but MX records only tell senders which hosts accept inbound mail for the domain; they say nothing about which hosts are authorised to send it.'),
('n-q-343','netplus',3,'3.4','mcq',2,'A newly built mail server can send mail internally, but several external providers reject its messages because its sending address has no reverse lookup. What must be created?','["A PTR record in the reverse lookup zone for the server address", "A second A record for the server in the forward zone", "A CNAME alias pointing to the server host name", "An additional NS record for the domain"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Reverse lookups map an address back to a name using PTR records in the in-addr.arpa reverse zone, and many mail receivers treat a missing PTR as a spam signal - note that this zone is usually controlled by whoever owns the address block. Adding another A record is the tempting fix, but forward records resolve names to addresses and are never consulted by a reverse query.'),
('n-q-344','netplus',3,'3.4','multi',3,'Which TWO statements correctly describe DNS query behaviour? (Choose two.)','["A client sends a recursive query to its configured resolver and expects a final answer or an error", "The resolver sends iterative queries, following referrals from a root server to a TLD server to the authoritative server", "A root server returns the final address record for the requested host", "Iterative queries are sent by the client directly to the authoritative server", "A CNAME record ends the resolution process without any further lookup"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1]'::jsonb,'The division of labour is the point: the client asks once, recursively, and the resolver does the walking, querying each level iteratively and following referrals until it reaches the authoritative server. Expecting a root server to return the host record is the classic misunderstanding - roots only refer the resolver to the correct top-level domain servers, and a CNAME triggers a further lookup of the target name rather than ending resolution.'),
('n-q-345','netplus',3,'3.4','multi',3,'Which TWO statements about network time services are correct? (Choose two.)','["NTP uses UDP port 123 and a stratum hierarchy, typically delivering millisecond-level accuracy across a network", "PTP can deliver sub-microsecond accuracy but generally depends on hardware timestamping and PTP-aware network devices", "Stratum 0 refers to the NTP server that clients are configured to query", "PTP replaces NTP on general-purpose servers and workstations by default", "NTP authenticates and encrypts all time messages by default"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1]'::jsonb,'NTP is the general-purpose choice, cheap to deploy and accurate enough for logging, Kerberos and certificate validation; PTP is the precision option for financial, industrial and broadcast timing and needs supporting hardware along the path. Calling the queried server stratum 0 is the frequent slip: stratum 0 is the reference clock itself, such as a GPS or atomic source, and the server directly attached to it is stratum 1.'),
('n-q-346','netplus',3,'3.4','ordering',3,'Place the steps of a typical client name resolution in the order they occur.','["The client checks its local DNS resolver cache for a valid, unexpired entry", "The client checks the local hosts file for a static mapping", "The client sends a recursive query to its configured DNS resolver", "The resolver queries a root server, then the TLD server, then the authoritative server", "The answer is cached by the resolver and the client for the length of the record TTL"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1, 2, 3, 4]'::jsonb,'Resolution starts locally and widens only when it must: cache first because it costs nothing, then the hosts file for administratively fixed mappings, then a recursive query outward, with the resolver walking the hierarchy and everyone caching the result until the TTL expires. Assuming the query goes straight to a DNS server is the common error, and it is why a stale hosts file entry can silently override a perfectly correct public record.'),
('n-q-347','netplus',3,'3.5','mcq',2,'A contractor using an unmanaged personal laptop needs access to a single internal web application. Company policy forbids installing corporate software on non-company devices. Which remote access method fits?','["A clientless VPN through a browser-based TLS portal", "A full client-to-site IPsec VPN using the corporate VPN client", "A site-to-site VPN between the contractor home router and the corporate gateway", "An out-of-band console connection to the application server"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A clientless VPN terminates TLS in a standard browser and publishes specific applications through a portal, so nothing is installed and the contractor reaches only what is published - a good match for limited third-party access. A full client-to-site VPN is the tempting default answer, but it requires the forbidden client installation and typically grants network-layer access far broader than one web application.'),
('n-q-348','netplus',3,'3.5','mcq',3,'Security requires that all traffic from remote workers, including general web browsing, pass through the corporate web filter and inspection stack. How should the client VPN be configured?','["Full tunnel, so every packet from the client is sent through the corporate gateway", "Split tunnel, so only corporate-bound traffic uses the VPN", "Clientless, so all access happens inside the browser", "Site-to-site, so the home network is joined to the corporate network"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Full tunnel forces all client traffic through the corporate gateway, which is the only configuration that lets the corporate security stack see and filter internet-bound browsing. Split tunnel is the tempting choice because it performs better and reduces load on the concentrator, but by design it sends internet traffic straight out of the local connection where corporate inspection never sees it.'),
('n-q-349','netplus',3,'3.5','mcq',2,'After enabling split tunnelling to reduce VPN concentrator load, what security consequence should the team expect?','["Internet-bound traffic from remote clients bypasses corporate filtering, inspection and logging", "Corporate-bound traffic is sent unencrypted across the internet", "Remote clients lose the ability to resolve internal DNS names", "The concentrator can no longer authenticate remote users"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Split tunnelling changes only which traffic enters the tunnel: corporate traffic stays encrypted, but everything else leaves the device directly, so the corporate proxy, DNS filtering and logging never observe it. Believing that corporate traffic becomes unencrypted is the common misreading - the tunnel itself is unchanged, and authentication and internal name resolution continue to work.'),
('n-q-350','netplus',3,'3.5','mcq',1,'An audit finds that switches are managed over Telnet. Which change addresses the finding?','["Disable Telnet and manage the switches over SSH", "Move Telnet to a non-standard high port number", "Restrict Telnet to the management VLAN only", "Require longer passwords on the Telnet accounts"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'SSH provides encrypted, integrity-protected sessions with device authentication via the host key, which is why it replaces Telnet for device management entirely. Moving Telnet to a different port is the tempting mitigation, but the port number does not change the protocol - credentials and configuration output still cross the wire in cleartext, and longer passwords are no help when the password itself is readable in the capture.'),
('n-q-351','netplus',3,'3.5','mcq',3,'A mistyped VLAN change on a remote branch switch has cut off all IP reachability to it. Which access method is most likely to still let an administrator recover the device?','["A console connection reached through an out-of-band console server or cellular management appliance", "An SSH session to the switch management IP address", "The switch web GUI, reached over the WAN link", "An API call to the switch over the production network"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Out-of-band access uses a path that does not depend on the production network - a console server or cellular appliance attached to the serial console port - which is precisely why it survives a configuration error that breaks IP reachability. SSH to the management address is the tempting answer because it is the normal recovery tool, but it is in-band and was broken by the same change.'),
('n-q-352','netplus',3,'3.5','mcq',2,'What defines out-of-band management?','["Management access travels over a path that is independent of the production data network", "Management traffic is encrypted end to end", "Management access is limited to read-only monitoring commands", "Management is performed only during approved maintenance windows"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'The defining characteristic is path independence: a separate console server, dedicated management circuit or cellular link that keeps working when production forwarding does not. Encryption is the tempting answer because secure management is strongly associated with the topic, but an encrypted SSH session over the production network is still in-band, and an unencrypted serial console is still out-of-band.'),
('n-q-353','netplus',3,'3.5','mcq',2,'All administrative sessions into a restricted server segment must pass through a single hardened, heavily logged host that requires multi-factor authentication. What is this host called?','["A jump box (bastion host)", "A VPN concentrator", "A forward proxy for outbound user traffic", "A syslog collector"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A jump box is a hardened intermediary that is the only permitted entry point into a protected segment, which collapses many administrative paths into one that can be patched, monitored and gated with MFA. A VPN concentrator is the tempting choice since it also controls remote entry, but it terminates tunnels at the network edge and does not by itself constrain which internal hosts an administrator may then reach.'),
('n-q-354','netplus',3,'3.5','mcq',2,'A switch has just been unboxed and has no IP address configured. How does a technician perform the initial configuration?','["Connect a laptop to the console port with the appropriate serial or rollover cable and use a terminal emulator", "Connect over SSH to the factory default management address", "Browse to the device web GUI through the uplink port", "Issue REST API calls to the device over HTTPS"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Console access is local and requires no IP configuration at all, which is why it is the standard method for initial provisioning and for recovery. SSH to a factory default address is the tempting answer because some vendors do ship one, but that assumption fails on the many devices that have no IP address until one is configured - and every network-based method shares the same dependency.'),
('n-q-355','netplus',3,'3.5','mcq',3,'A team must apply an identical VLAN and ACL change to two hundred switches with a verifiable record of what was changed on each. Which management method is most appropriate?','["Programmatic access through the device REST API over HTTPS with token-based authentication", "Logging into each device web GUI and clicking through the same screens", "A script that automates mouse and keyboard input against the web GUI", "A console session to each device in turn"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'An API returns structured, machine-readable responses and can be driven from version-controlled automation, so the change is repeatable, reviewable and auditable across hundreds of devices. Automating clicks against the GUI is the tempting middle ground, but it is brittle - a firmware update that moves a button silently breaks the automation, and it produces no reliable record of success or failure.'),
('n-q-356','netplus',3,'3.5','multi',2,'Which TWO reasons make Telnet and HTTP unacceptable for managing network infrastructure? (Choose two.)','["Credentials and configuration data cross the network in cleartext and can be captured by anyone on the path", "Neither protocol authenticates the device to the administrator, so an on-path attacker can impersonate it", "Telnet uses TCP port 22, which is normally blocked at the firewall", "HTTP is unable to carry device configuration commands", "Both require dedicated client software to be installed on the workstation"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1]'::jsonb,'The two failures are confidentiality and authenticity: everything typed and displayed is readable in transit, and there is no server certificate or host key to prove you are talking to the real device rather than an interposed attacker. The port claim is wrong in a tempting way - Telnet is TCP 23, and 22 belongs to its secure replacement SSH.'),
('n-q-357','netplus',3,'3.5','multi',3,'Which THREE statements about a jump box are accurate? (Choose three.)','["It should be hardened and expose only the management protocols that are genuinely required", "It concentrates administrative access so sessions can be logged, recorded and gated with MFA", "If it is compromised, the attacker gains a launching point toward every system it can reach", "It removes the need to patch the systems that sit behind it", "It encrypts data at rest on the servers it provides access to", "It makes network segmentation unnecessary"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1, 2]'::jsonb,'A jump box trades many entry points for one that is easier to defend and monitor, but that concentration cuts both ways: it becomes a high-value target whose compromise yields broad reach, so it must be minimal, patched and tightly monitored. Believing it substitutes for patching or segmentation is the dangerous misconception - it is a control point that depends on those defences, not a replacement for them.'),
('n-q-358','netplus',3,'3.5','multi',2,'Which THREE practices reduce the risk attached to network device management access? (Choose three.)','["Restrict management sessions to a dedicated management network or a jump box using a management ACL", "Require multi-factor authentication with named individual accounts through centralised AAA", "Replace Telnet and HTTP management with SSH and HTTPS", "Publish the management interface on a public address so it stays reachable during an outage", "Share one enable password across the whole network team for speed", "Use SNMPv2c community strings as the credential for administrative logins"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1, 2]'::jsonb,'The three correct practices limit who can reach the management plane, make every action attributable and revocable centrally, and protect the session itself in transit. Exposing management to the internet is the dangerous inversion of the outage argument - that is what out-of-band management is for - and shared passwords and community strings destroy attribution while being trivially reusable once captured.'),
('n-q-359','netplus',3,'3.5','multi',3,'Which THREE are genuine out-of-band management access paths? (Choose three.)','["A serial cable from a technician laptop directly to the device console port", "A console (terminal) server on a dedicated management network reaching each device console port", "A cellular modem attached to an out-of-band management appliance in the rack", "An SSH session to the device across the production data VLAN", "The device web GUI reached over the corporate LAN", "REST API calls to the device across the production network"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1, 2]'::jsonb,'All three correct options reach the device by a path that does not traverse the production data network, so they remain usable when routing, VLANs or the WAN circuit are broken. The other three are in-band regardless of how they are secured - SSH, HTTPS and API access are excellent management methods, but they fail together with the network they depend on.');
insert into flashcards (id, cert, domain, objective, deck, front, back) values
('n-f-300','netplus',3,'3.1','core','What is the difference between a physical and a logical network diagram?','A physical diagram shows the hardware reality: device locations, rack and closet placement, cable runs, patch panel and switch port assignments. A logical diagram shows the addressing and forwarding reality: VLAN IDs, IP subnets, routing adjacencies, firewall zones and traffic flow. The same network needs both, because neither drawing answers the questions the other one is for.'),
('n-f-301','netplus',3,'3.1','core','What does a rack diagram record, and which decision does it support?','A front and rear elevation of each cabinet showing which rack unit each device occupies, plus power feeds, circuit loading, weight and cabling. It answers whether a new device physically fits and whether the cabinet has power and cooling headroom - the capacity question a logical diagram cannot answer.'),
('n-f-302','netplus',3,'3.1','core','What is a cable map, and what problem does it solve?','Documentation of which patch panel port terminates which wall jack or horizontal run, and which switch port it patches into, tied to a consistent labelling scheme. Without it, tracing one circuit means physically toning out cables; with it, a move or a fault becomes a lookup.'),
('n-f-303','netplus',3,'3.1','core','What is IP address management (IPAM) and what does it prevent?','A system of record for subnet allocations and individual address assignments, usually integrated with DHCP and DNS. It prevents overlapping subnet allocations between sites, duplicate address assignments, and the situation where nobody can say what a given address is used for or whether it is free.'),
('n-f-304','netplus',3,'3.1','acronym','SLA','Service-Level Agreement - a contractual commitment to measurable service levels such as availability percentage, response time and time to restore, with a defined remedy (typically service credits) when a target is missed.'),
('n-f-305','netplus',3,'3.1','core','What figures does a network service-level agreement typically commit to?','Availability as a percentage over a period (99.9 percent allows roughly 43 minutes of downtime per month), a response time to a reported fault, a target time to restore service, and often latency, jitter or packet-loss ceilings on the circuit. It also states the remedy when a target is missed - which is what makes it an agreement rather than a marketing claim.'),
('n-f-306','netplus',3,'3.1','core','What is a wireless site survey, and what does a heat map show?','A survey measures or models RF coverage for a specific building: predictive surveys model from floor plans before deployment, on-site surveys take real readings after. The heat map overlays signal strength on the floor plan in colour, exposing dead zones, excessive overlap and poor AP placement. It is about your own coverage - use a spectrum analyser to hunt non-Wi-Fi interference.'),
('n-f-307','netplus',3,'3.1','acronym','EOL','End-of-Life - the date after which the vendor no longer sells or manufactures a product. Support and patching usually continue for a defined period afterwards, so EOL is a procurement and refresh-planning signal rather than an immediate security event.'),
('n-f-308','netplus',3,'3.1','acronym','EOS','End-of-Support (also end-of-service-life) - the date after which the vendor issues no further patches, firmware or technical assistance. This is the security deadline: after EOS, every newly discovered vulnerability in the device is permanent.'),
('n-f-309','netplus',3,'3.1','core','Why does end-of-support matter more than end-of-life for risk management?','EOL only means you can no longer buy it; the device still receives fixes. After EOS no security patches are ever issued again, so risk grows with every new vulnerability disclosed. Past EOS your only options are to replace the device or wrap it in compensating controls such as strict segmentation and tighter monitoring.'),
('n-f-310','netplus',3,'3.1','core','What belongs in a proper device decommissioning procedure?','Sanitise the configuration, keys, certificates and cached credentials; remove entries from IPAM, DNS, DHCP, monitoring and backup jobs; unpatch and relabel the physical ports; update rack diagrams, cable maps and the asset inventory; reclaim licences and support contracts; and dispose of the hardware per policy with a record of destruction.'),
('n-f-311','netplus',3,'3.1','core','What does software and firmware life-cycle management cover on network gear?','Tracking the code version on every device, applying security patches and bug fixes on a defined schedule, testing firmware upgrades in a lab or on a pilot device first, and scheduling upgrades through change management with a rollback image available. Version sprawl across supposedly identical devices is itself a defect - it produces inconsistent behaviour that is very hard to troubleshoot.'),
('n-f-312','netplus',3,'3.1','core','What are the stages of a change management process?','Request (scope, purpose, impact and risk) - approval (change advisory board or delegated approver) - schedule into a maintenance window - implement with the rollback plan ready - validate and test the result - document and close. Emergency changes follow an abbreviated path but are still reviewed and documented after the fact.'),
('n-f-313','netplus',3,'3.1','core','What is a rollback plan and why is it mandatory for a network change?','A documented, tested sequence for returning to the last known good state, including where the prior configuration and firmware image are stored and how long the reversal takes. It is mandatory because it lets you set a decision point inside the maintenance window: if validation has not succeeded by time X, you revert rather than improvise.'),
('n-f-314','netplus',3,'3.1','core','What is a maintenance window, and who defines it?','A pre-approved period, agreed with the business, during which disruptive work may be performed - normally when usage and revenue impact are lowest. The business defines it, not the network team, because it exists to bound the impact on users; work that overruns the window is normally rolled back rather than continued.'),
('n-f-315','netplus',3,'3.1','core','What is the difference between the production configuration and a backup configuration?','The production (running) configuration is what the device is executing right now, held in volatile memory. The backup configuration is a stored copy, either the device non-volatile startup configuration or an archive on an external repository. A change applied only to the running configuration disappears at the next reboot.'),
('n-f-316','netplus',3,'3.1','core','What is a baseline (golden) configuration, and what is configuration drift?','The approved standard configuration for a device role - hardening, logging, VLANs, management access - that every device of that type should match. Drift is the accumulated divergence of live devices from that standard through one-off changes. Configuration monitoring detects drift by continually comparing production configurations against the baseline.'),
('n-f-317','netplus',3,'3.1','core','Why does an accurate asset inventory underpin life-cycle management?','You cannot plan a hardware refresh or assess EOL and EOS exposure for models you do not know you own. The inventory ties model, serial number, firmware version, support contract and installed location together, which is what turns a vendor end-of-support bulletin into a costed replacement project rather than a surprise.'),
('n-f-318','netplus',3,'3.2','acronym','MIB','Management Information Base - the hierarchical database definition of the objects an SNMP agent exposes. The manager references an object by its OID (object identifier) to read a specific value such as an interface octet counter or CPU utilisation.'),
('n-f-319','netplus',3,'3.2','core','SNMP polling versus traps: what does each catch and what does each miss?','Polling is manager-initiated (Get requests to UDP 161 on the agent) at a fixed interval - excellent for trending, but blind to anything that starts and ends between two samples. Traps are agent-initiated notifications sent to UDP 162 on the manager the moment an event occurs - immediate, but unacknowledged, so a dropped trap is silently lost. Mature designs use both.'),
('n-f-320','netplus',3,'3.2','core','What is the difference between an SNMP trap and an SNMP inform?','A trap is sent once over UDP with no acknowledgement - if it is lost, neither side knows. An inform (available from SNMPv2c onward) is acknowledged by the manager and retransmitted by the agent until confirmed, at the cost of extra traffic and agent state. Use informs for notifications you cannot afford to miss.'),
('n-f-321','netplus',3,'3.2','core','How do SNMP v1, v2c and v3 differ?','v1 is the original, community-string based. v2c keeps the cleartext community string but adds GetBulk and informs. v3 adds the user-based security model with three levels: noAuthNoPriv, authNoPriv (authentication and integrity), and authPriv (adds encryption). Only v3 protects credentials and payloads - v1 and v2c community strings are readable in any capture.'),
('n-f-322','netplus',3,'3.2','core','What is an OID in SNMP?','An object identifier: a dot-separated numeric path through the MIB tree that uniquely names one queryable value, such as the inbound octet counter for a specific interface index. The manager gets or walks OIDs; graphing tools translate them to friendly names using loaded MIB files.'),
('n-f-323','netplus',3,'3.2','core','How do NetFlow, IPFIX and sFlow differ as flow-data sources?','NetFlow is the Cisco-originated standard that accounts for full flows keyed on source and destination address, ports and protocol. IPFIX is the IETF standardisation of NetFlow v9, with templated, extensible fields. sFlow is vendor-neutral and works by statistical 1-in-N packet sampling in hardware, so it scales to very high speeds but reports estimates. All three send from an exporter on the device to a central collector, and none of them retain payloads.'),
('n-f-324','netplus',3,'3.2','core','What is port mirroring (SPAN), and when is a network TAP better?','Port mirroring copies traffic from selected ports or VLANs to a monitor port feeding an analyser or IDS. It is free and configurable, but the mirror destination is easily oversubscribed - mirroring several busy ports into one drops frames silently, and the switch deprioritises mirroring under load. A passive TAP is inline hardware that copies every bit at line rate without depending on switch resources, which is why it is preferred for evidentiary or high-rate capture.'),
('n-f-325','netplus',3,'3.2','core','What does a SIEM add over a plain syslog collector?','A syslog collector centralises and stores messages (severity 0 emergency through 7 debug). A SIEM normalises messages from many source types, correlates events across them, applies detection rules, raises alerts, and provides retention and search for investigation. Centralising logs makes them available; correlating them makes them useful.'),
('n-f-326','netplus',3,'3.2','core','How should baseline metrics drive anomaly alerting?','Collect metrics over a period long enough to cover the real business cycle - the nightly backup, the weekly report run, the monthly payroll - then alert on deviation from that expected pattern rather than on a single static number. A fixed threshold on cyclical traffic either fires on every routine peak (producing alert fatigue and ignored alerts) or is raised so high that it misses real saturation.'),
('n-f-327','netplus',3,'3.3','acronym','RPO','Recovery Point Objective - the maximum acceptable amount of data loss, expressed as time measured backwards from the incident. It sets how frequently you must back up or replicate.'),
('n-f-328','netplus',3,'3.3','acronym','RTO','Recovery Time Objective - the maximum acceptable time a service may be unavailable, measured forwards from the incident. It sets how capable, and how expensive, your recovery infrastructure must be.'),
('n-f-329','netplus',3,'3.3','acronym','MTTR','Mean Time To Repair (or Restore) - the measured average time taken to return a failed component to service. It is a maintainability metric derived from history, not a target the business sets.'),
('n-f-330','netplus',3,'3.3','acronym','MTBF','Mean Time Between Failures - the measured average operating time between failures of a repairable system. It is a reliability metric: higher is better, and it is used to compare candidate hardware and to predict spares requirements.'),
('n-f-331','netplus',3,'3.3','core','RPO versus RTO: what does each measure, and in which direction?','RPO looks backwards from the incident and answers how much data you can afford to lose - a fifteen-minute RPO means the last good copy must be at most fifteen minutes old. RTO looks forwards and answers how long the service may stay down. They are independent: you can have a very short RTO with a poor RPO (fast failover to stale data) or the reverse.'),
('n-f-332','netplus',3,'3.3','core','MTTR versus MTBF: what is the practical difference?','MTBF is how often a thing breaks (reliability); MTTR is how long it takes to fix once it has broken (maintainability). Availability improves by raising MTBF or lowering MTTR - buying more reliable hardware, or stocking spares and improving procedures. Both are measured averages, which is what separates them from the RPO and RTO targets.'),
('n-f-333','netplus',3,'3.3','core','Which design decisions does the RPO drive?','Backup and replication frequency, and the technology needed to achieve it. A 24-hour RPO is met by nightly backups; a 1-hour RPO needs hourly snapshots or log shipping; a near-zero RPO needs synchronous or continuous replication. Faster restore hardware does not improve RPO at all - that is an RTO investment.'),
('n-f-334','netplus',3,'3.3','core','Which design decisions does the RTO drive?','The recovery architecture: which site type you need (cold, warm or hot), whether you run active-active or active-passive clustering, whether failover is automatic or manual, and how much pre-staged capacity you pay to keep idle. A short RTO is bought with standing infrastructure and automation.'),
('n-f-335','netplus',3,'3.3','core','What is a cold site?','An alternate facility with conditioned space, power, cooling and network access, but little or no installed equipment or data. After a declaration you must procure or ship hardware, install it, configure it and restore data - the cheapest option to hold and by far the longest recovery time, typically days to weeks.'),
('n-f-336','netplus',3,'3.3','core','What is a warm site?','An alternate facility that already has hardware, circuits and a working baseline configuration, but not current production data. Recovery means restoring the most recent backup and completing cutover, usually taking hours up to about a day. It is the middle of the cost-versus-speed trade-off and the common enterprise compromise.'),
('n-f-337','netplus',3,'3.3','core','What is a hot site?','A fully provisioned, configured and continuously updated duplicate of production, kept current by near-real-time replication so it can take over in minutes with little or no data loss. It is the only option for a very short RTO combined with a very short RPO, and the most expensive to build and maintain.'),
('n-f-338','netplus',3,'3.3','core','How do you choose between cold, warm and hot sites?','Work from the metrics, not the label. The RTO sets how ready the site must be (days tolerable means cold, hours means warm, minutes means hot) and the RPO sets how current its data must be. Cost rises steeply as both shrink, so the honest exercise is agreeing with the business what an hour of downtime and an hour of lost data actually cost.'),
('n-f-339','netplus',3,'3.3','core','What is an active-active high-availability design, and what is its sizing trap?','All nodes carry production traffic simultaneously, sharing load and providing failover. The trap is capacity planning: each node must be able to carry the entire load alone, because when one fails the survivor inherits everything. Two nodes each running at 60 percent are not truly redundant - they are one failure away from 120 percent and dropped traffic.'),
('n-f-340','netplus',3,'3.3','core','What is an active-passive high-availability design, and what does it trade away?','One node carries all production traffic while the standby stays synchronised but idle, assuming the active role on failure. It is simpler and post-failover behaviour is predictable, because one node was always carrying the full load. The trade is that you pay for capacity that is never used in normal operation.'),
('n-f-341','netplus',3,'3.3','core','What is a tabletop exercise and what does it find?','A discussion-based walkthrough of a scenario with the people who would actually respond, touching no production or recovery systems. It is cheap, carries no operational risk, and is very effective at exposing unclear ownership, missing contact paths, undocumented dependencies and unrealistic assumptions - the human failures rather than the technical ones.'),
('n-f-342','netplus',3,'3.3','core','What is a validation test, and what does it prove that a tabletop cannot?','A validation test actually exercises the recovery mechanism - failing over to the recovery site, restoring from backup, and verifying that services, data currency and dependencies genuinely work. It is the only way to catch the technical failures a discussion never surfaces: expired certificates, missing firewall rules, backups that never restored cleanly, and undocumented dependencies on the failed site.'),
('n-f-343','netplus',3,'3.4','acronym','SLAAC','Stateless Address Autoconfiguration - the IPv6 mechanism in which a host forms its own global address from the prefix in a router advertisement plus a locally generated interface identifier. No server keeps any binding, which is what makes it stateless.'),
('n-f-344','netplus',3,'3.4','core','What is a DHCP scope?','The pool of addresses a DHCP server may lease on a particular subnet, together with its mask, lease duration and the options handed out with it. One scope corresponds to one subnet; the server picks the correct scope for a request based on the receiving interface or the relay agent address.'),
('n-f-345','netplus',3,'3.4','core','What is the difference between a DHCP exclusion and a DHCP reservation?','An exclusion is a block of addresses inside the scope that the server will never hand out - used to protect addresses assigned statically to routers, printers and servers. A reservation binds one specific address to one client identifier (usually a MAC address) so that client always receives the same address while still obtaining its options via DHCP.'),
('n-f-346','netplus',3,'3.4','core','What does the DHCP lease time control, and when does a client renew?','It is how long a binding remains valid. The client attempts renewal with its current server at T1, half the lease, and if that fails it broadcasts to rebind with any server at T2, roughly seven-eighths of the lease. Short leases suit high-turnover networks such as guest Wi-Fi; long leases reduce traffic on stable networks but leave stale bindings holding addresses.'),
('n-f-347','netplus',3,'3.4','core','Which DHCP options should a technician recognise?','Option 3 default gateway (router), option 6 DNS servers, option 15 DNS domain name, option 42 NTP servers, option 51 lease time, and options 66 and 67 for the TFTP server name and boot file used in PXE and IP phone provisioning. Clients that get a valid address but cannot route or resolve names almost always have a missing or wrong option 3 or option 6.'),
('n-f-348','netplus',3,'3.4','core','Why is a DHCP relay (IP helper) needed, and what does it actually do?','DHCP clients start with a broadcast, and routers do not forward broadcasts, so a subnet with no local server would get no leases. The relay configured on the router interface receives the broadcast, records its own interface address in the giaddr field and forwards it as a unicast to the DHCP server - which is also how the server knows which scope to serve.'),
('n-f-349','netplus',3,'3.4','core','How does SLAAC actually build an address?','The host sends a router solicitation; the router replies with a router advertisement carrying the /64 prefix. The host appends an interface identifier - either derived from its MAC using EUI-64 (splitting the 48-bit MAC, inserting fffe, and flipping the seventh bit) or a randomised privacy identifier - then runs duplicate address detection before using the address.'),
('n-f-350','netplus',3,'3.4','core','What do the M and O flags in an IPv6 router advertisement mean?','M (managed) set tells hosts to obtain their addresses from a stateful DHCPv6 server. O (other configuration) set tells hosts to obtain other settings such as DNS servers from DHCPv6 while still forming their own address. Both clear means rely entirely on the router advertisement. SLAAC with the O flag set is the common stateless DHCPv6 design.'),
('n-f-351','netplus',3,'3.4','core','Which IPv6 fundamentals differ from IPv4 on a local link?','Every interface automatically has a link-local address in fe80::/10 used for neighbour discovery and routing adjacencies. There is no broadcast at all: NDP replaces ARP using ICMPv6 neighbour solicitation and advertisement sent to multicast groups, with ff02::1 all-nodes and ff02::2 all-routers. Duplicate address detection runs before any address is used.'),
('n-f-352','netplus',3,'3.4','core','Beyond A, AAAA, CNAME and MX, which DNS record types does N10-009 expect you to know?','TXT carries free-form text used for SPF, DKIM and domain-ownership verification. NS delegates a zone to its authoritative name servers. PTR maps an address back to a name in the reverse zone. SOA holds the zone authority parameters including the serial number and default TTLs. SRV advertises the host and port for a specific service.'),
('n-f-353','netplus',3,'3.4','core','What is the difference between a forward and a reverse lookup zone?','A forward zone resolves names to addresses using A and AAAA records - the everyday direction. A reverse zone resolves addresses back to names using PTR records, and lives under in-addr.arpa for IPv4 or ip6.arpa for IPv6. Reverse zones are normally delegated to whoever owns the address block, which is why a hosting customer often cannot create their own PTR record.'),
('n-f-354','netplus',3,'3.4','core','What is the difference between a recursive and an iterative DNS query?','A client sends one recursive query to its resolver and expects a final answer. The resolver then does the work with iterative queries: it asks a root server, which refers it to the TLD servers, which refer it to the authoritative servers for the domain, which return the record. Root and TLD servers never return the host record itself - only referrals.'),
('n-f-355','netplus',3,'3.4','core','What are internal versus external DNS, and what do DoH and DoT change?','Split-horizon DNS serves different answers to internal and external clients, so private hostnames and internal addresses are never published on the internet. DNS over HTTPS (DoH, port 443) and DNS over TLS (DoT, port 853) encrypt client queries - good for privacy, but they can bypass the internal resolver and defeat DNS-based filtering and logging, so enterprises usually control them explicitly.'),
('n-f-356','netplus',3,'3.4','core','How do NTP, PTP and NTS differ?','NTP (UDP 123) uses a stratum hierarchy - stratum 0 is the reference clock such as GPS or atomic, stratum 1 is directly attached to it, and each level down is one step further - delivering millisecond accuracy, which is fine for logging, Kerberos and certificate validation. PTP (IEEE 1588) delivers sub-microsecond accuracy using hardware timestamping and PTP-aware switches, for finance, industrial control and broadcast. NTS adds cryptographic authentication to NTP so clients can verify the time source.'),
('n-f-357','netplus',3,'3.5','core','What is a clientless VPN and what are its limits?','A TLS portal reached in a standard browser: the user authenticates to the gateway and reaches published applications with no VPN software installed. It suits contractors and unmanaged devices because access is restricted to what is published. The limit is that it mainly serves web-enabled applications - arbitrary network protocols and thick clients generally still need a full VPN client.'),
('n-f-358','netplus',3,'3.5','core','Split tunnel versus full tunnel: what is the trade-off?','Full tunnel sends all client traffic through the corporate gateway, so everything is inspected, filtered and logged, at the cost of concentrator bandwidth and added latency for internet browsing. Split tunnel sends only corporate-bound traffic through the tunnel, which performs far better but leaves internet traffic entirely outside corporate inspection.'),
('n-f-359','netplus',3,'3.5','core','Why do many remote-access VPNs use TLS rather than IPsec?','IPsec relies on ESP and IKE, which are often blocked by hotel, airport and public networks and can be awkward through NAT without NAT traversal. A TLS-based VPN rides TCP 443, which is open essentially everywhere and traverses NAT cleanly, so it connects from places IPsec cannot. IPsec remains the norm for site-to-site tunnels where both endpoints are under your control.'),
('n-f-360','netplus',3,'3.5','core','What does SSH provide for device management?','An encrypted, integrity-protected session on TCP 22 that also authenticates the device to you via its host key, so an interposed attacker is detected. It supports key-based authentication rather than reusable passwords, and carries secure file transfer (SCP and SFTP) and port forwarding over the same connection. It is the direct replacement for Telnet.'),
('n-f-361','netplus',3,'3.5','core','Why are Telnet and HTTP unacceptable for managing network devices?','Both are cleartext: credentials, configuration output, community strings and keys are readable by anyone on the path. Neither authenticates the device to the administrator, so an on-path attacker can impersonate it and harvest or alter what you send. Changing the port number or lengthening passwords fixes nothing - replace them with SSH (TCP 22) and HTTPS (TCP 443).'),
('n-f-362','netplus',3,'3.5','core','When is console access the only workable management method?','Initial configuration of a device that has no IP address yet, password and configuration recovery, changing the boot image, and recovering from a change that broke IP reachability. It uses a serial or rollover cable from a laptop to the console port with a terminal emulator, and works entirely independently of the network.'),
('n-f-363','netplus',3,'3.5','core','What is the difference between in-band and out-of-band management?','In-band management travels over the same production network that carries user traffic - convenient and cheap, but it fails together with the network it depends on. Out-of-band management uses a completely separate path, so it survives a routing error, a VLAN mistake or a WAN outage. Encryption is not the distinction: encrypted SSH over production is in-band, and a plain serial console is out-of-band.'),
('n-f-364','netplus',3,'3.5','core','How is out-of-band management implemented in practice?','A console (terminal) server in each rack connected to every device console port and reached over a dedicated management network; a dedicated out-of-band management interface on the device itself; a separate management circuit; or a cellular modem in an out-of-band appliance so the site stays reachable when the WAN circuit is down.'),
('n-f-365','netplus',3,'3.5','core','What is the security risk of out-of-band management, and how is it controlled?','The OOB path deliberately bypasses the production network, so it also bypasses the firewalls, NAC and monitoring that protect it - an unsecured cellular modem or console server is a back door into every device it touches. Control it with strong authentication and MFA, dedicated credentials, an encrypted access path, strict source restrictions and full session logging.'),
('n-f-366','netplus',3,'3.5','core','What is a jump box (bastion host) and why use one?','A hardened intermediary host that is the only permitted entry point into a protected segment: administrators connect to it, then from it to the target systems. It collapses many administrative paths into one that can be patched, monitored, session-recorded and gated with MFA, and it means the protected systems need no direct inbound access from user networks.'),
('n-f-367','netplus',3,'3.5','core','How should a jump box be hardened, and what is its inherent risk?','Minimal installed software, only the required management protocols exposed, aggressive patching, MFA on every login, dedicated privileged accounts, full session logging or recording, and tight restrictions on which sources may reach it. The inherent risk is concentration: because it can reach everything behind it, its compromise gives an attacker exactly that reach, so it is never a substitute for segmentation or patching.'),
('n-f-368','netplus',3,'3.5','core','GUI versus CLI for device management: when does each win?','A GUI is quicker to learn, good for visualisation, dashboards and occasional tasks, and harder to make a catastrophic typo in. A CLI is precise, scriptable, consistent across devices of the same platform, and produces output that can be captured and compared - which is why configuration backups, bulk changes and documented recovery procedures are CLI-based.'),
('n-f-369','netplus',3,'3.5','core','What does API-based management add over the GUI or CLI?','A documented REST interface over HTTPS with token authentication returns structured data (typically JSON) and accepts structured changes, so configuration can be driven from version-controlled automation. That makes bulk changes repeatable across hundreds of devices, provides a reviewable record of exactly what was requested, and lets the network be managed by the same tooling as the rest of the infrastructure.'),
('n-f-370','netplus',3,'3.5','core','Is a dedicated management VLAN out-of-band management?','No. A separate management VLAN is good practice - it isolates management traffic from user traffic and limits who can reach device management planes - but it still rides the production switching and routing infrastructure, so it is in-band. If a routing or VLAN error breaks the network, the management VLAN goes with it; true out-of-band needs a physically independent path.'),
('n-f-371','netplus',3,'3.5','core','What does a VPN concentrator do?','It terminates many VPN tunnels at the network edge: handling key exchange and encryption in hardware, authenticating users against RADIUS or a directory, applying posture and access policy, and assigning tunnel addresses. It is the aggregation point for client-to-site access, so its capacity and its policy configuration determine both remote-worker performance and how much of the internal network a remote user can actually reach.'),
('n-f-372','netplus',3,'3.5','core','Beyond using SSH and HTTPS, how should management access itself be restricted?','Restrict management sessions to a specific management network or jump box using a management ACL or control-plane policy, require MFA and named individual accounts rather than a shared enable password, use centralised AAA (RADIUS or TACACS+) so access can be revoked in one place, and never expose device management interfaces directly to the internet.'),
('n-f-373','netplus',3,'3.5','core','What does a site-to-site VPN over the internet give you, and what does it not?','It gives confidentiality, integrity and authentication between two gateways, joining the networks so users at each site need no client software and notice nothing. It does not give the transport guarantees of a private circuit - there is no bandwidth commitment, no latency or jitter ceiling and no SLA on the internet path, which is why latency-sensitive traffic across a VPN still needs QoS at the ends and realistic expectations in the middle.');
